[template]
help = '''
.github/workflows/bites.yml

TEMPLATE for the workflow. scripts/bootstrap.sh copies it into a project and fills
in the two placeholders on the `uses:` lines below: the repository that holds the actions
(default imdebating/bites) and the version (default v1). Until then it is not a valid
workflow.

Thin wrapper: All of the work is done by the bites action; this file only decides WHEN
to run and WHAT the jobs may do. Do not edit the steps. To change the version, change the
ref after @ (a moving major tag such as v1, an exact tag, or a full commit SHA), or
re-run the bootstrap script with --workflow-version (-v).

Optional settings go under `with:` next to `full`: max-chars, read-cap, include and
exclude (see the README). They override a bites.toml in the project. Give them to the
bootstrap script as -c/--config KEY=VALUE (-c), so that running it again keeps them.

Two jobs:
  bites    on every push (and by hand):  large files and apply patch files.
  cleanup  when a branch is deleted: delete that branch's bites/<branch> output branch.

Opt-in per branch: nothing is built unless a branch named bites/<branch> exists.
A push-triggered run uses the copy of this file in the pushed commit, so every branch that
should build needs its own copy. The bootstrap script adds it to each branch you name (or
to all of them with --all-branches), next to the bites/<branch> output branch. A branch created
later gets the file when it is created from a branch that has it; otherwise run the
bootstrap script again. A delete-triggered run always uses the copy on the DEFAULT branch,
so the cleanup job needs the file on the default branch only.
Push to <branch> (applied patches), to bites/<branch> (bites) and delete bites/<branch>
(cleanup). Nothing else.
'''
input_full = "Rebuild and re-segment every large file instead of only changed ones"

